The fstab (file system table) file contains information about which file systems
to mount at which path at boot. It essentially works like the mount command and
takes similar parameters.

Depending on the number and types of file systems in use, the syntax can be a
bit complex and difficult to read. Furthermore the amount of available options
makes a full explanation of the layout at this point unpractical (and
unnecessary). Arch Linux includes the genfstab script which generates an fstab
file for the new system based on the mounted file systems at the time of its
execution. If all partitions and subvolumes are properly mounted, no further
modifications of the fstab file should be necessary.

We will open the fstab file next so you can check if genfstab generated the file
correctly. As it is usually quite reliable, you don't need to worry if you do
not understand each option in the file. For a general understanding, see the
individual columns below. Note that each row in fstab represents a block device
whose file system will be mounted at boot.
fstab makes use of these columns separated by at least one space:
    - 1. file system: identifier for the file system to be mounted; can be
        prefixed by UUID= or LABEL= as an alternative to direct paths
    - 2. dir: path to the mount point of the volume
    - 3. type: the type of file system used by the volume
    - 4. options: mount options; mount uses the -o flag for setting these
    - 5. dump: whether dump (a backup utility not overly popular anymore) should
        consider this file system for backups
    - 6. pass: when/if a file system check is conducted by fsck at boot, this
        column determines the order in which the file systems are checked